`bindTouch()` calls `UI.setTouch(true)` at boot, so touch UI is visible even on the title screen! That explains "TORCH"/"RUN" showing behind the title and caught/win screens. On mobile, touch buttons are always visible, including on the title screen, pause, cutscenes, caught, win. That's a real polish bug, likely unnoticed because screenshots were of gameplay.

Fix: only show touch UI during active gameplay, and permanently hide on win/caught/cutscene. Implement HUD visibility gating:

In ui.js:
```
_touch: false,
_touchOn: false,
setTouch(v) { this._touch = !!v; this.el['touch-ui'].classList.toggle('hidden', !v || !this._hudVisible); },
showHud(v) { this._hudVisible = !!v; this.el.hud.classList.toggle('hidden', !v); if (!this._touch) return; this.el['touch-ui'].classList.toggle('hidden', !v); },
```
Initialize `_hudVisible=false`, `_touch=false`.

Order issue at boot: bindTouch → setTouch(true), _hudVisible false → hidden. Correct: title has no touch UI.

beginSkip: UI.hideScreens(); UI.showHud(true) → sets _hudVisible true; then UI.setTouch(this.touch) → shows. Good, but between showHud(true) and setTouch, if _touch was false nothing shows until setTouch. Fine.

beginLockdown: UI.showHud(false) → hides touch. runCutscene also showHud(false). showWin/caught: showHud(false) hide. Resume from pause: togglePause → UI.showHud(true) re-shows touch only if _touch. Need to check pause handling: does it call showHud(true) on resume and is _touch true? setTouch called at beginPlay. yes.

`noteOpen`: does it hide HUD? note popup open maybe HUD stays; not relevant.

Caught flow: onDetected → showHud(false) hides touch; good. Retry reloads.

Also mobile touch-ui might currently intercept pointer events on screens (though z-index). Hiding fixes.

Implement now.

Then CSS short-height win fix. Also check caught screen fits with the real layout (353 in 390) — fits but tight; touch buttons hidden helps. Add a mild short-height media query for caught too? The caught tip at 353 leaves 18px margins, acceptable. On 360-height phones (older Android landscape), 353 might overflow slightly. Reduce h2 on short screens. Let's add:

```
@media (max-height: 480px) {
  #caught-screen h2{font-size:clamp(44px,14vh,92px)}
  #caught-tip{margin:10px auto 16px;font-size:10.5px}
  .caught-inner{padding:10px}
  #win-screen h1{...}
  ...
}
```
But h2 clamp: 14vh of 390=54.6; CSS clamp with vh works. Use max-height 520 to include 390.

For win:
```
